WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.580
Speaker 1: Hey, David, how could Republicans
convince young people to start voting for

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:05.580
them?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.430
Yeah, so there's still this really big imbalance
in the 2020 US election.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:15.600
Presidential, 60 percent of voters, eighteen
to twenty nine voted for Joe Biden, 60 percent.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.939
Only 36 percent voted for Donald Trump.

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:22.869
I think a lot of this is sort of a culture
war issue related.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:27.090
And I'll explain it to you in this way.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:32.840
The Republican Party historically has had
their sort of more religious and cultural

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:39.500
issues and then their policy platforms, the
policy platform of low taxes and low regulation.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.530
It's not really appealing to young people.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.910
And that is not a draw for young people to
the Republican Party.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:53.360
When the country was more religious, stuff
like being against gay marriage and being

00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:01.300
against abortion, maybe did more to pull some
young people in to the Republican Party.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:09.580
But over time, we are seeing that even so-called
conservative young people tend to have significantly

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.070
more liberal views when it comes to economic
issues than they did 15 or 20 years ago.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.909
The Occupy Wall Street movement was a part
of that, and other things have affected that.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:20.040
They are increasingly less religious.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.270
So like gay marriage, abortion are not big
issues for most young people.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:29.940
So what does the Republican have to offer
them?

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.720
Maybe the new culture war issues could potentially
work.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.380
So, for example, if somebody like Madison
Cawthorn, a young Republican elected official

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.850
from North Carolina, not South Carolina, as
I misstated last week, if Madison Cawthorn

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:50.100
can get young people really fixated on the
new cultural issues, which include, you know,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:58.781
the platforming and free speech and censorship,
cancel culture and that type of thing, maybe

00:01:58.781 --> 00:02:02.549
that's a way that the Republican Party could
capture more of the young vote.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:04.200
That's a possibility.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:13.180
The problem, as I see it, is that the Republican
Party on policy doesn't really have much right

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:20.010
now other than opposed Democrats cut welfare
programs and cut taxes.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.970
They are against the Affordable Care Act,
but they don't really have a solution.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:28.180
And young people are probably aware that if
ACA goes, if I'm under twenty six, I may not

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.880
be able to stay on my parent's insurance.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.630
So there's no interesting policy ideas.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:36.730
And so as interesting as the idea of we're
going to fight cancer culture and we're going

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.970
to fight against policing of speech and platforming
and all the stuff that might be interesting

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.900
to some young people and might pull them towards
the Republican Party if they care at all about

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.910
policy, there's going to be nothing there.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:53.281
So I think that unless there's a wholesale
change within the Republican Party, they're

00:02:53.281 --> 00:02:54.940
going to keep hemorrhaging young voters.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:01.770
Now, that being said, still, given how ridiculous
Republican policy has been towards anything

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.989
that young people would care about.

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:10.120
It's shocking they still got 36 Trump, Trump
still got 36 percent of the 18 to 29 nine

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.770
vote doing everything wrong, they still got
thirty six percent of the vote.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.300
So this is one of those things where on the
one hand, the Republican Party is doing everything

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.069
wrong to try to appeal to young people.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:23.930
And the things I think could help them appeal
to more young people are not things they seem

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:24.930
interested in doing.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:25.930
So that seems terrible.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.730
Spells disaster.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.550
But yet, on the other hand, they still got
36 percent of the 18 to 29 vote.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:38.440
So only some small tweaks could probably make
them quite competitive or at least closer

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.410
to competitive among that group.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:48.340
Give me your sense of whether there is a way
to maintain something that is the modern Republican

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.390
ideology that would appeal to young people.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:04:53.740
I'm not really sure that there is.

